// Reading what a program said, and what that makes the value.
//
// `process.capture` returns standard output - but only when the program
// succeeded, because what a program printed on its way to failing is not an
// answer. The result is `Observed<String>`: nobody verified it, nobody
// approved it, and a program printed it.
//
//     sic plan examples/capture.sic
//     sic run examples/capture.sic
//
// Passing that value straight to `process.exec` does not compile. A string a
// program printed must not decide what the next program runs, which is the
// oldest injection there is. `approve` is the way through, and it puts a
// person on the record: see docs/design/output.md.
allow {
    process.capture "/bin/echo" args ["sic:"];
}

fn main() -> Observed<String> {
    return process.capture("/bin/echo", ["sic:", "read back"]);
}
